================================================================================
 H0 / sigma8 TENSIONS - COSMOLOGICAL MCMC PIPELINE: COMBINATIONS GUIDE
================================================================================
Folder: Cosmological-Analysis-Modified-and-Parametrised-Gravity-main/
Everything below was read from the code (run_config.py, parameterisations.py,
fR.py, likelihood.py, main.py, data_loader.py).  If you change a prior or add a
model, update this file.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1. HOW A RUN IS DEFINED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A run is one choice from each of three switches at the top of main.py:

  PARAMETERISATION   background model (section 2)
  FR_MODEL           gravity model (section 3); "GR" = no modification
  USE_SNIA / USE_CC / USE_BAO / USE_CLUSTER   dataset toggles (section 6)

plus FR_FIXED_PARAMS, FR_Z_INIT and FR_OMEGA_M_ANCHOR (f(R) runs only).

Sampled vector (emcee):   [ H0, *parameterisation params, *f(R) params ]

 * FR_MODEL = "GR":  the parameterisation IS the background, E^2(z) = H^2/H0^2.
 * FR_MODEL != "GR": H(z) comes from the f(R) field equations with matter only
   (or matter + the parameterisation's dark energy for "hu_sawicki_de").
   The parameterisation then only supplies Omega_m (and, for power_law and
   R_log_R, the initial conditions).
 * In f(R) runs Omega_m is NOT sampled: it is degenerate with alpha, so it is
   pinned at FR_OMEGA_M_ANCHOR (0.3).  The physical present-day matter fraction
   Omega_m/u(0) is reported as a derived quantity.  H0 is always the true
   present-day value (E^2(0) = 1).

Output names:  run tag = "<param>" or "<param>__<fR>__<pinned>=<value>"
  e.g. LCDM, wCDM__R_lambda_R2__alpha=1e-09, LCDM__starobinsky
  chain_<tag>_<datasets>.npy      graphs/<tag>/...      (datasets e.g. snia+cc+bao+clu)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 2. PARAMETERISATIONS  (parameterisations.py)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
All priors are uniform with hard walls (strict inequalities).  H0: (50, 100).

 name                      sampled besides H0        priors / notes
 ------------------------  ------------------------  --------------------------
 LCDM                      Om                        Om (0.05, 0.70)
 wCDM                      Om, w0                    w0 (-3, 0)
 wCDM_fixed_Omega_m        w0                        Om hard-wired to 0.3
 CPL                       Om, w0, wa                wa (-5, 5)
 wowaCDM                   Om, w0, wa                IDENTICAL to CPL (same E^2,
                                                     same prior); only the start
                                                     point differs
 JBP                       Om, w0, wa                wa (-5, 5)
 HDE                       Om, c                     c (0.1, 3); constant
                                                     w_eff = -1/3 - 2/(3c),
                                                     i.e. -7 ... -0.55
 IDE                       Om, xi                    xi (-1, 1); see section 8
 param_Hubble_fixed_delta  Om0, alpha                Om0 (0.05, 0.95),
                                                     alpha (-2, 4); delta fixed
                                                     (FIXED_DELTA = 0.05)
 param_Hubble_fixed_alpha  Om0, delta                delta (-2, 2); alpha fixed
                                                     (FIXED_ALPHA = 0.17)
 Custom_Universal          Om0                       delta, alpha both fixed

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 3. f(R) MODELS  (fR.py)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Every modified model needs f_RR > 0 (stability); it is enforced in the prior.

 FR_MODEL       f(R)                         free params / priors (closed
                                             intervals unless stated)
 -------------  ---------------------------  ----------------------------------
 GR             f = R                        none (dark energy from the
                                             parameterisation)
 power_law      R + alpha R^n                alpha [-10, 10], n [0.1, 2],
                                             n != 1; alpha*n*(n-1) > 0, i.e.
                                             alpha < 0 for n < 1, alpha > 0
                                             for n > 1
 R_log_R        R (1 + alpha ln R)           alpha [0, 3] (alpha > 0)
 R_lambda_R2    R - 2 Lambda + alpha R^2     Lambda [0, 20], alpha (0, 1e-4]
 starobinsky    R + alpha R^2                alpha (0, 1e-4]   NO Lambda
 hu_sawicki     R - 2 Lambda R/(R+Lambda b)  Lambda (0, 20], b (0, 2)   (p = 1)
 hu_sawicki_de  hu_sawicki + the             same as hu_sawicki; Lambda is an
                parameterisation's DE        EXTRA constant, needs <~ 0.3

 FR_MODEL       how H(z) is obtained                 default FR_Z_INIT
 -------------  -----------------------------------  ---------------------------
 power_law      ODE (LSODA), ICs from parameterisation   40
 R_log_R        ODE (LSODA), ICs from parameterisation   40
 R_lambda_R2    closed form, 2nd order in alpha          2.5
 starobinsky    closed form, 2nd order in alpha          2.5
 hu_sawicki(_de) ODE (Radau), own attractor ICs          10

 R_lambda_R2 / starobinsky: FR_Z_INIT is the highest redshift where the
 expansion is trusted.  It must exceed the highest requested z (BAO 2.36, SNIa
 grid 2.37); below ~2.4 the code raises an error.  Parameters are also rejected
 when the estimated truncation error exceeds 1e-3 (alpha ~ 9e-5).

 Runtime rejections (any ODE model): u <= 0, R <= 0, blow-up |u'/u| > 1e3,
 too many right-hand-side evaluations (stiff), or u(0) outside (1e-2, 1e2).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 4. COMPATIBILITY MATRIX  (what run_config.py accepts)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 OK = valid and meaningful
 o  = valid, but the parameterisation's extra parameters (w0, wa, c) are unused
      or negligible: they just add prior-dominated dimensions.  Use LCDM.
 !! = valid, but results are unreliable (section 8)
 ?? = valid, but a physics problem was found (section 8)
 -- = rejected with a ValueError

                           GR  power  R_log  R_lam  Staro  Hu-Saw  Hu-Saw
                               _law   _R     _R2    binsky         _de
 LCDM                      OK  OK     !!     OK     OK     OK      --
 wCDM                      OK  o      !!     o      o      o       OK
 wCDM_fixed_Omega_m        OK  o      !!     o      o      o       OK
 CPL                       OK  o      !!     o      o      o       OK
 wowaCDM                   OK  o      !!     o      o      o       OK
 JBP                       OK  o      !!     o      o      o       OK
 HDE                       OK  o      !!     o      o      o       OK
 IDE                       ??  --     --     --     --     --      --
 param_Hubble_fixed_delta  OK  --     --     --     --     --      --
 param_Hubble_fixed_alpha  OK  --     --     --     --     --      --
 Custom_Universal          OK  --     --     --     --     --      --

 Rules enforced:
  A. FR_MODEL = "GR": any of the 11 parameterisations.
  B. Any modified model needs a parameterisation with a conserved-matter Om
     (excludes IDE, param_Hubble_*, Custom_Universal) -> 7 remain.
  C. hu_sawicki_de additionally rejects LCDM (a constant fluid duplicates
     Lambda) -> 6 remain.
  D. FR_FIXED_PARAMS names must exist for the chosen model; pinning n = 1 is
     rejected; if all f(R) parameters are pinned the prior is checked at once.

 Count: 11 + 5 x 7 + 6 = 52 valid combinations
        (20 OK, 24 'o', 7 '!!', 1 '??').

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 5. WHICH ONES TO ACTUALLY RUN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Background-only comparison (FR_MODEL = "GR"):
   LCDM, wCDM, wCDM_fixed_Omega_m, CPL (or JBP), HDE, param_Hubble_*.
   Skip wowaCDM (= CPL).  Do not trust IDE until section 8 is resolved.
 Modified gravity with a cosmological constant:
   LCDM + R_lambda_R2, LCDM + hu_sawicki, LCDM + power_law (0 < n < 1).
 Modified gravity with a dark-energy parameterisation:
   {wCDM, CPL, JBP, wowaCDM, HDE, wCDM_fixed_Omega_m} + hu_sawicki_de.
 Minimal reference model (no dark energy at all):
   LCDM + starobinsky (section 9).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 6. DATASETS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Any non-empty subset of the four toggles (15 combinations); with none enabled
 main.py aborts.

 dataset                       N      z range       likelihood
 ----------------------------  -----  ------------  -------------------------
 SNIa (Pantheon+SH0ES)         1701   0.001-2.26    full STAT+SYS covariance,
                                                     constant offset M_B
                                                     marginalised analytically
 CC (cosmic chronometers)      31     0.07-1.97     diagonal errors only
 BAO                           6      0.24-2.36     used as H(z) points
 Clustering                    21     0.10-0.73     used as H(z) points

 Consequences:
  * SNIa carry NO information on H0 here: the marginalisation removes any
    constant offset in mu, and H0 only enters mu as a constant.  A SNIa-only
    run returns the prior U(50, 100) for H0.  In joint fits H0 is constrained
    by CC + BAO + clustering only; SNIa act through Om and the shape
    parameters.  (For Q_DMAP the SNIa-only chi2_min is still well defined, but
    do not count H0 as a constrained parameter.)
  * BAO alone (6 points) leaves 2 degrees of freedom for a 4-parameter model.
  * Clustering alone stops at z = 0.73.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 7. TYPICAL SETTINGS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 main.py switchboard examples:
   PARAMETERISATION = "LCDM";  FR_MODEL = "GR"             (baseline)
   PARAMETERISATION = "LCDM";  FR_MODEL = "R_lambda_R2";  FR_FIXED_PARAMS = {"alpha": 1e-9}
   PARAMETERISATION = "wCDM";  FR_MODEL = "hu_sawicki_de"; FR_FIXED_PARAMS = {"b": 0.9}
   PARAMETERISATION = "LCDM";  FR_MODEL = "starobinsky";  FR_FIXED_PARAMS = {}

 Commands (inside the venv):
   python 04_diagnostics/check_fR.py       self-tests of the f(R) solvers
   python 01_run_configuration/main.py      MCMC run for the current switchboard
   python 02_plotting/make_plots.py --list  list chains and plotting options
   python 02_plotting/make_plots.py --plots comparison --no-show
   git apply patches/<name>.patch           apply a patch

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 8. KNOWN PROBLEMS  (found while reading the code; none are fixed here)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * IDE has no LCDM limit.  rho_de ~ 3/(3+xi)(1+z)^3 + xi/(3+xi)(1+z)^(-xi), so
   xi = 0 gives E^2 = (1+z)^3 (matter only), and the walkers start at xi = 0.
   Within xi in (-1, 1) the second term never exceeds ~1/3 of the total.
 * R_log_R has no GR matter-era attractor: E^2(z) changes by 10-50 % with
   FR_Z_INIT (fR.py docstring).  Do not trust its results.
 * power_law with n > 1 is allowed by the prior, but the GR-like early matter
   era that the initial conditions assume only exists for 0 < n < 1.
 * R_log_R: alpha = 0 passes the (closed) prior but gives f_RR = 0.
 * R_lambda_R2 and starobinsky: alpha is unconstrained by background data (its
   posterior is the prior cut at ~9e-5); R_lambda_R2 is degenerate with LCDM.
 * With R_lambda_R2 / hu_sawicki / starobinsky the parameterisation's w0, wa, c
   are unused: e.g. wCDM__R_lambda_R2 reports w0 spread over most of its prior.
 * CPL, JBP, HDE, IDE clamp E^2 with max(E^2, 1e-9) instead of rejecting
   E^2 <= 0; bad regions are only penalised through a huge chi2.
 * wowaCDM duplicates CPL; HDE is wCDM reparametrised (w_eff set by c).
 * CC uses diagonal errors (no off-diagonal covariance).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 9. STAROBINSKY MODEL  (FR_MODEL = "starobinsky")
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 f(R) = R + alpha R^2, no cosmological constant, one free parameter alpha.
 It is the R_lambda_R2 closed form at Lambda = 0:

     u(z) = x + (27/2) alpha x^2 - 729 alpha^2 x^3 + O(alpha^3),
     x = Om (1+z)^3,   E^2(z) = u(z)/u(0)     (anchor units, Om = 0.3)

 The tt equation was checked symbolically: the residual vanishes through
 O(alpha^2).  Prior: alpha in (0, 1e-4] (f_RR = 2 alpha > 0).

 What to expect: there is no dark energy of any kind.  For alpha <= 9e-5 the
 R^2 term changes E^2 by at most 1.3 % relative to (1+z)^3 for z <= 2.4, so the
 expansion is matter-dominated at all times (q0 ~ 0.5, no acceleration).
 Best fit on snia+cc+bao+clu (optimiser, not MCMC):

     LCDM (GR):          H0 = 67.38, Om = 0.3153, chi2 = 1797.3
     LCDM + starobinsky: H0 -> 50 (prior wall), chi2 = 2925.3
     Delta chi2 = +1128 for N = 1759 data points

 The model is a minimal reference, not a competitive late-time model.  Use
 LCDM as the companion; any other parameterisation only supplies Om.
================================================================================


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
10. PLOTTING SAVED MCMC CHAINS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Plotting uses saved chain_*.npy files: it does NOT run MCMC again. The plotting
scripts live in Cosmological-Analysis-Modified-and-Parametrised-Gravity-main/.
Run the commands below from that directory with the project virtual environment
active (or replace "python" with the path to its Python executable).

First, see the available plot types, saved chains, dataset/run-mode tags, and
the parameters sampled by each chain:

  python make_plots.py --list

If no options are given, make_plots.py opens an interactive menu in a terminal.
For reproducible/non-interactive use, specify plots on the command line:

  # One model: posterior corner plot and walker traces
  python make_plots.py --plots corner traces --models LCDM

  # Compare several models with 1D posterior histograms
  python make_plots.py --plots hist --models LCDM wCDM --params H0 Omega_m

  # Compare contours for H0 and w0, using wCDM as the reference
  python make_plots.py --plots contour_vs_ref --models LCDM wCDM --pair H0 w0 --reference wCDM

  # All comparison plot types, without opening GUI windows
  python make_plots.py --plots comparison --no-show

  # Select the dataset tag explicitly and set the walker count for traces
  python make_plots.py --plots traces --run-mode snia+cc+bao+clu --walkers 64

Plot keys and groups:
  corner, traces, potential, omega_components   per-model plots
  hist, box, violin, contour_vs_ref, contour_all, hubble   model comparisons
  --plots model       all per-model plot types
  --plots comparison  all comparison plot types
  --plots all         every plot type

Useful options:
  --list                   list available chains, run modes, and plot types
  --models TAG [TAG ...]   choose chain/run tags; omit to use all matching chains
  --run-mode TAG           choose the dataset suffix in chain filenames
  --params NAME [NAME ...] parameters for hist/box/violin (default: H0 matter)
  --pair X Y               contour axes (default: H0 matter)
  --reference TAG           reference chain for contour_vs_ref
  --walkers N               walker count used when sampling (trace plots only)
  --bins N                  number of bins for posterior histogram overlays
  --no-show                save figures without opening interactive windows
  --root PATH              project directory containing chains, or a directory
                           whose chains/ subdirectory contains them
  --graphs-dir PATH        output directory for generated figures

Output locations:
  Per-model figures are saved under graphs/<run-tag>/.
  Multi-model figures are saved under graphs/Model_Comparisons/.
  --graphs-dir changes the figure output directory; --root changes the project
  root used to discover chains and, by default, the graphs/ output location.
  Figures are saved as PDF. Plot failures and model/plot incompatibilities are
  summarized in the terminal; a plot that does not apply to a model is skipped.

Notes:
  * Run tags must match the tags printed by --list exactly, including any f(R)
    model and fixed-parameter suffixes.
  * The run mode must match the suffix in the chain filename. If no chains are
    found, use --list to see available modes and pass the correct --run-mode.
  * f(R) chains have Omega_m anchored rather than sampled. Prefer parameters
    that are actually sampled by every model being compared (for example H0,
    alpha_fR, or Lambda_fR). Parameters absent from a model are omitted from
    that comparison.
  * Walker traces require the same --walkers value used to create the chain.
    The flattened .npy chain does not store this value; an incorrect count can
    scramble the apparent walker traces even if the length is divisible by it.
  * The legacy compare_posteriors.py, plot_hubble_diagram.py, and
    plot_walk_traces.py entry points remain available. For new workflows,
    prefer make_plots.py so plot selection, model selection, and run mode are
    controlled consistently in one place.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
11. PROJECT FOLDER LAYOUT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Scripts are grouped by workflow. Run commands from the repository root
(the directory containing this readme). Each script adds the project folders
to Python's import path, so sibling modules remain importable after the move.

  01_run_configuration/
      main.py                 Edit the model, f(R) choice, datasets and MCMC settings
      parameterisations.py    Background cosmological parameterisations
      fR.py                   Modified-gravity models and background solvers
      run_config.py           Run tags, sampled parameters and compatibility

  02_plotting/
      make_plots.py           Recommended plotting entry point
      plot_selector.py        Plot registry and dispatcher
      plotting.py             Per-model figures
      comparison_plots.py     Multi-model figures
      compare_posteriors.py, plot_hubble_diagram.py, plot_walk_traces.py
                              Legacy plotting entry points

  03_data_and_likelihood/
      data_loader.py, likelihood.py, datasets/
                              Observational data and likelihood evaluation

  04_diagnostics/
      check_convergence.py    Chain convergence diagnostic
      check_fR.py             f(R) solver self-tests

  05_results_and_reconstruction/
      results_io.py           Numerical result exports
      Modified_Gravity_Models/
                              Standalone Friedmann / f(R) reconstruction scripts

Generated figures go to graphs/. MCMC chains are saved under chains/ and
are intended to remain local; chain_*.npy is ignored by Git. Existing chain
files at the repository root are still discoverable by the plotting tools.

Examples from the repository root:
  python 01_run_configuration/main.py
  python 02_plotting/make_plots.py --list
  python 02_plotting/make_plots.py --plots corner traces --models LCDM
  python 02_plotting/make_plots.py --plots hist --models LCDM wCDM --params H0
  python 04_diagnostics/check_fR.py
